These scripts can be used for generating a timeseries dataset from MSG files.
The different files have the following usages.

crop_MSG_timeseries.py
-> script to crop and save timeseries from MSG files contained in EWC data bucket.

upload_MSG_timeseries_to_data_bucket.py 
-> script to automatically upload the generated timeseries to a dedicated EWC data bucket 
(needs to be created first).

download_MSG_timeseries_from_data_bucket.py
-> script to automatically download the generated timeseries from the dedicated EWC data bucket 
when you need them for training.



-------- How to run the timeseries cropping script --------

The script crop_MSG_timeseries.py can be run as a standalone script.
You need to specify the years, months, and days for which you want to generate timeseries
from the MSG data stored in the EWC S3 bucket.

All the parameters for the timeseries generation are specified in the main section of the script
(around line 330). You can modify them as needed.

Parameter descriptions:
- years: List of years to process.
- months: List of months to process.
- days: List of days to process.
- bucket_name: Name of the S3 bucket containing the MSG data.
- n_frames: Number of frames in each timeseries sample.
- max_daily_offset: Maximum random offset (as a fraction of n_frames; between 0 and 1) to introduce randomness
  in the starting times of the timeseries in the beginning of each day.
- cropsize: Size of the spatial crop (in pixels).
- max_spatial_overlap: Maximum allowed overlap (as a fraction of cropsize; between 0 and 1) between different
  spatial crops to ensure diversity.
- max_cropping_attempts: Maximum number of attempts to find a valid crop without NaN values.
- out_path: Directory where the generated timeseries crops will be saved.
- out_basename: Base name for the output files.
- verbose: If True, prints progress information.


The main function to generate the timeseries dataset is
construct_timeseries_dataset(bucket_name, years, months, days, 
                             n_frames=8, max_daily_offset=None, 
                             cropsize=100, max_spatial_overlap=0.25, max_cropping_attempts=10, 
                             out_path=None, out_basename=None, 
                             verbose=True)
in crop_MSG_timeseries.py



-------- General procedure of construct_timeseries_dataset --------

- For each specified day, the script retrieves the MSG data from the S3 bucket.
- The starting time is randomly selected if max_daily_offset is specified.
- A while loop iterates through the day to extract timeseries samples.
- In each iteration:
    - It searches for a time window of the specified number of frames that does not contain NaN values.
    - If a valid time window is found, it randomly selects spatial crops of the specified size 
      within each quadrant, ensuring that the crops do not exceed the maximum allowed overlap among each other.
    - For each crop, the cloud mask is added as an additional parameter, as well as applied to the 10.8 µm channel (IR_108_cm).
    - The valid timeseries crops are then saved to the specified output directory with the given base name. 
- If the found timeseries window has not enough timestamps then we reached the end of the data 
  contained in the daily file. This trailing timeseries is kept for the next day processing.
- When moving on to the next day, if there is a trailing timeseries from the previous day, 
  it is prepended to the data of the new day to ensure continuity.
- The process continues until all specified days have been processed.

